JAN (Japanese Article Number) — format provenance and usability
==========================================================================

This file records where the JAN symbology comes from, what governs it, and
the basis on which this project implements and redistributes it.

It is an ENGINEERING INVENTORY, NOT LEGAL ADVICE. Items marked [TO VERIFY]
have not been independently confirmed by the author.


ORIGIN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

JAN is a NUMBERING convention, not a distinct symbology. A JAN barcode is
an ordinary EAN-13 whose GS1 country-code prefix falls in the range
assigned to Japan (45x or 49x). The number is administered by GS1 Japan;
the symbol is EAN-13 under GS1's rules — the same relationship this
project already implements for ISBN (`licenses/isbn.license`).


SPECIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

ISO/IEC 15420 — EAN/UPC bar code symbology specification (the symbol).
GS1 General Specifications, GS1 AISBL — freely published; redistribution
terms are GS1's own [TO VERIFY]
JIS X 0501 — the Japanese standard referencing JAN/EAN, as adopted
domestically. [TO VERIFY]

Specification TEXT is copyrighted by its publisher. The SYMBOLOGY it
describes is not — an encoding scheme is a system, not a work of
authorship. This project implements the symbology; it does not reproduce,
redistribute or excerpt any specification document.


PATENT STATUS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Inherits EAN-13's position; no patent encumbrance is known for the
numbering convention or the symbol used to print it. [TO VERIFY]


TRADEMARK
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

No live trademark restriction is known on implementing JAN encoding.
"JAN" is a standard designation administered by GS1 Japan. [TO VERIFY]

A trademark does not restrict implementing a symbology. It restricts
branding — how a product names and presents itself.


IMPLEMENTATION BASIS IN THIS PROJECT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

JAN is implemented here as a thin prefix-validating wrapper over this
project's existing EAN-13 encoder — no new digit grammar, check digit
algorithm or symbol structure. Reading follows the same shared-decoder
pattern already used for ISBN: a JAN read is reported as `ean13`, with the
`jan` id available as a `formats` filter that requires the 45/49 prefix.
No source code or constant table from any other barcode implementation is
copied or shipped.


CONCLUSION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

On the basis of the references below, JAN is understood to be freely
implementable and redistributable at the same trust bar already applied to
ISBN, and this project's implementation of it is distributed under the MIT
License with no additional restriction.

References relied upon:
  - ISO/IEC 15420, ISO/IEC
  - GS1 General Specifications, GS1 AISBL — freely published;
    redistribution terms are GS1's own [TO VERIFY]
  - JIS X 0501, Japanese Standards Association [TO VERIFY]

